## SECTION 4: SERP ANALYSIS

**competitor_gaps** (demand-validated on 2+ sources):
- A stage guide that keeps the reader's decision in view: "what do I do at THIS stage, and when does home care stop being safe." DermNet gives per-stage treatment but in clinical tone; London Foot & Ankle and Advanced Foot Specialists give a definition and nothing actionable. Straits Podiatry and a Birmingham practice cover it but with a clinic-sales angle.
- Skin-tone-aware description. Redness is the standard sign in every top page; only Biology Insights notes infection can show as deeper brown or purplish on darker skin. Cleveland Clinic touches it once ("redness or darkening"). PAA and clinical writing confirm the gap.
- A fourth and fifth stage. The consumer pages stop at three. The clinical literature (Mozena, five stages; Scholz, 1A to 5B) and one case report describe advanced chronic stages with skin bridging and complete nail-fold envelopment. No consumer page tells the reader that stage 3 is not the ceiling.
- Look-alikes: paronychia (whole-nail-fold, fast onset) and pincer nail (both edges curl). Only one aggregator page covers this. It stops readers self-diagnosing the wrong stage.
- How fast it moves. Nobody gives the reader a realistic sense of how quickly stage 1 becomes stage 2. Expert quote below supplies the honest answer: "can start without much pain before rapidly developing into something very sore."

**missing_semantic_territory:** nail fold, granulation tissue ("proud flesh"), hypergranulation, lateral nail fold hypertrophy, callus at the nail edge as the first sign, pseudo-ingrown nail of the newborn, pincer nail, involuted nail, cellulitis warning signs (spreading redness, red streaks, fever), skin bridging, chronic stage, darker skin infection signs, "looks worse each day."

